WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.380
Command-line arguments are commonly handled as C strings, and that means ordinary C string discipline still applies.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:16.600
The program should not assume a value will fit into an internal buffer, produce a safe formatted message, or compare correctly under unclear encoding rules.

3
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.680
Length checks should happen before copying, formatting, or concatenation.

4
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:30.420
The parser should distinguish source length from destination capacity and should preserve room for a terminating null character when an internal buffer is meant to hold a C string.

5
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.040
A command-line value can be longer than the program author expected.

6
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.280
Fixed-size buffers need special care.

7
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:47.460
If a tool copies arguments into fixed arrays, the code should define maximum accepted lengths, check them before the copy, and handle too-long values with a clear error.

8
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:54.920
Silent truncation can change resource names, identifiers, paths, or mode values.

9
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.080
Encoding and character assumptions should be documented.

10
00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:09.220
A tool may expect simple ASCII-like option names, UTF-8 text, path strings in the platform's usual representation, or opaque bytes carried as arguments.

11
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:15.800
The parser should define what characters are allowed for each field and how whitespace or unusual bytes are handled.

12
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.560
Comparison rules also depend on the field.

13
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:26.420
Some options are case-sensitive, some are not, and some should be matched only after a documented normalization step.

14
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:34.040
Safe string handling keeps length, capacity, encoding, and comparison behavior aligned with the option contract.
